@@s001 Graphs: basic vocabulary
@@s002 ora:what I count now,ok:the count
@@s003 An undirected graph: nodes (circles) and edges (lines). No arrows, so each edge holds in both directions.
@@s004 Nodes: <b>a, b, c, d, e</b>. So <code>n = 5</code>.
@@s005 Edges: ab, bc, ad, be, ce, de. Count one by one: there are <b>six</b>, so <code>m = 6</code>.
@@s006 The <b>degree</b> of a node is the number of edges that touch it. The degree of <b>b</b> is 3 (ab, bc, be): read it as "how many neighbours it has".
@@s007 ora:the line I read,ok:the neighbour found
@@s008 The graph: four nodes, four edges. We want to know: <b>the neighbours of b</b>, and <b>is there the edge a–d?</b>.
@@s009 Adjacency list: for each node, its list. The neighbours of <b>b</b> are those written in its row: a, c, d. Reading them costs the degree of b.
@@s010 Edge a–d: I go to the row of <b>a</b> and look for <b>d</b>. I find it: the edge is there. The cost is the degree of a.
@@s011 Same question on the matrix: a 4 × 4 table with 1 where there is an edge. Reading the cell (a, d) costs <b>O(1)</b>. But the table has <code>n²</code> cells even if there are few edges.
@@s012 Graphs: basic vocabulary
@@s013 A graph is a set of connected objects. Before any algorithm you need four words: node, edge, degree, path. Every page of this atlas uses them.
@@s014 Nodes and edges
@@s015 A <b>graph</b> is a pair <code>G = (V, E)</code>. <code>V</code> is the set of <b>nodes</b> (vertices), <code>E</code> is the set of <b>edges</b>. An edge connects two nodes. Write <code>n = |V|</code> for the number of nodes and <code>m = |E|</code> for the number of edges: they are the two parameters with which almost every algorithm is measured.
@@s016 A graph is <b>undirected</b> if the edge <code>{u, v}</code> can be traversed in both directions, <b>directed</b> if the edge <code>u → v</code> goes only from <code>u</code> to <code>v</code>. A graph can have <b>weights</b> on the edges: one number per edge, for example a cost or a distance.
@@s017 The symbol that counts
@@s018 <code>|X|</code> means "how many elements <code>X</code> has". So <code>|V| = n</code> and <code>|E| = m</code>. In algorithms the time is always written in <code>n</code> and <code>m</code>, never as "the number of nodes" in words.
@@s019 A graph with five nodes and six edges: count them
@@s020 Two ways of storing it
@@s021 An algorithm must ask the graph two things: <i>what are the neighbours of a node?</i> and <i>is there the edge u–v?</i>. There are two classic structures, and the choice changes the cost.
@@s022 <b>Adjacency list</b>: for each node, a list of neighbours. Space <code>O(n + m)</code>. Scanning the neighbours of <code>u</code> costs <code>O(degree(u))</code>. Checking an edge costs the degree of a node.
@@s023 <b>Adjacency matrix</b>: an <code>n × n</code> table with 1 where there is an edge. Space <code>O(n²)</code>. Checking an edge costs <code>O(1)</code>, but scanning the neighbours costs <code>O(n)</code>.
@@s024 For almost all the algorithms of this course the adjacency list is the right choice: the graph is sparse, that is <code>m</code> is much smaller than <code>n²</code>.
@@s025 Same graph, two storages
